  <h1>Seat Sherpa vs Uber and Lyft for long-distance trips</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated September 2026 · Prices are typical ranges; live seat prices are on each route page</div>

  <p class="lede"><strong>Short answer:</strong> Uber and Lyft are ride-hailing: a commercial driver is dispatched to you and paid per mile and per minute, which is why San Francisco to Los Angeles runs $300 to $500 or more, if a driver accepts it at all. Seat Sherpa is carpooling: a driver who is already making the trip posts it ahead of time and you share their cost, capped at the IRS mileage rate split across seats and never more than $55 a seat.</p>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Seat Sherpa</th><th>Uber / Lyft</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What it is</td><td class="win">Scheduled carpool: book a seat on a trip the driver is taking anyway</td><td>On-demand ride-hailing inside metro areas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SF to LA, one person</td><td class="win">$30 to $55 + 5% fee</td><td>$300 to $500+</td></tr>
      <tr><td>LA to Las Vegas, one person</td><td class="win">$25 to $55 + 5% fee</td><td>$250 to $400+</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pricing</td><td class="win">Capped at the IRS mileage rate divided across seats; no surge</td><td>Per mile + per minute, surge at peak times</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Booking</td><td>Book ahead, usually a day to a week out; instant-book or request</td><td class="win">Minutes away, any time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Driver</td><td>Everyday driver: phone, license, vehicle and Stripe identity verified; public rating</td><td>Commercial TNC driver with background check</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Route</td><td>Fixed intercity route, exact pickup arranged in chat; some rides add stops along the way</td><td class="win">Anywhere the app operates</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payment</td><td class="win">Held by Stripe until your pickup code is entered; refunded if the ride does not happen</td><td>Charged at trip end</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Cost-sharing carpool, the driver’s personal policy applies</td><td class="win">Commercial policy while on trip</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Why the price gap is so large</h2>
  <p>An Uber driver has to be paid for the 380 miles to Los Angeles and, in practice, for the empty 380 miles home. Uber’s per-mile intercity pricing reflects that, and drivers frequently cancel long requests because the deadhead return is not worth it. A Seat Sherpa driver was going to Los Angeles regardless. The passenger is not paying for a driver’s time; they are covering a share of gas and wear, and the app enforces the cap on every listing.</p>

  <h2>When Uber or Lyft is still the better choice</h2>
  <p>Inside a city, for a trip under about 30 miles, or when you need a car in the next 10 minutes. Seat Sherpa does not do on-demand pickups and does not operate within a single metro. Many riders combine the two: Uber to the pickup point, carpool for the 300 miles, Uber the last few miles at the other end.</p>

